// A STATED LIMIT of differential fuzzing, kept executable: a shared miss is
// invisible, and so is semantic wrongness with matching bytes. The comparator
// asks whether the two implementations agree, never whether the agreement is
// correct.
//
// This program compiles on both sides to byte-identical C, so the fuzzer reports
// nothing about it - not because it is right, but because they concur. If a
// future round teaches the comparator to judge rather than to compare, this
// fixture goes red and forces that to be a decision rather than a side effect.
fn main() {
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < 3 {
        println(i);
        i = i + 1;
    }
}
